# Cindy on Fedora Linux: Installation and Update Guide

This guide documents my experience installing and updating [Cindy](https://cindy.app) on Fedora 44 Workstation with GNOME.

I initially launched Cindy from an extracted Debian package and later migrated to Cindy's managed per-user installation method using the installer bundled with the official package.

> **Tested environment:** Fedora 44 Workstation, GNOME, x86_64  
> **Initial version:** 0.1.120  
> **Managed installation:** 0.1.121  
> **Result:** Successfully launched on my system.

This is a community-contributed installation report based on my own experience. It does not imply that Fedora is an officially supported or fully tested distribution.

## Official Documentation

Before proceeding, check Cindy's official Linux installation guide for the latest instructions:

- [Cindy Linux installation guide](https://github.com/makecindy/cindy/blob/main/docs/linux.md)
- [Cindy website](https://cindy.app)

The commands and checksum below document the specific versions used during my installation. They should not be assumed to apply unchanged to future releases.

## 1. Initial Installation: Extracting the Debian Package

Cindy provided a Debian `.deb` package. Fedora does not use Debian packages as its native package format, but I was able to extract the package and launch the application manually.

### Install `dpkg`

```bash
sudo dnf install dpkg
```

### Extract the package

For my initial installation, I used Cindy 0.1.120:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/Downloads/cindy-inspect

dpkg-deb -x ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.120-amd64.deb \
  ~/Downloads/cindy-inspect
```

The application executable was located at:

```text
~/Downloads/cindy-inspect/usr/lib/cindy/Cindy
```

I launched it with:

```bash
cd ~/Downloads/cindy-inspect/usr/lib/cindy
./Cindy
```

**Result:** Cindy 0.1.120 launched successfully on my Fedora system.

This was a manual extraction, not a managed installation. When I attempted to use the application's built-in updater, Cindy reported that this installation could not update itself and recommended using a package manager or its Linux user-installation guide.

## 2. Preparing Fedora for the Managed Installation

The Cindy package included its own Linux user-installation scripts:

```text
usr/lib/cindy/resources/linux/install-user.sh
usr/lib/cindy/resources/linux/register-desktop.sh
```

I decided to migrate to the managed user installation instead of continuing to run the manually extracted application.

### Install `bsdtar`

The installation script required `bsdtar`, which was missing from my system.

I checked which Fedora package provided it:

```bash
sudo dnf provides '*/bsdtar'
```

Then installed it:

```bash
sudo dnf install bsdtar
```

I checked the other required utilities as well. The installation and desktop-registration scripts require several standard command-line tools, including `sha256sum`, `flock`, `desktop-file-validate`, `update-desktop-database`, and `xdg-mime`.

## 3. Downloading and Verifying Cindy 0.1.121

Cindy's official Linux update manifest identified version 0.1.121 and supplied the package path, expected size, and SHA-256 checksum.

I downloaded the package:

```bash
cd ~/Downloads

curl -fL \
  https://hotfix.cindy.app/cindy/app/linux-x64/cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb \
  -o cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb
```

I verified the download:

```bash
sha256sum cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb
```

The published checksum for this specific release was:

```text
b756663c3f30d6cf8a75d4d8ea38336842689a504b682d62c6dd3d6ba01e0663
```

**The calculated SHA-256 checksum matched the value in Cindy's official manifest.**

Always verify a downloaded package against the trusted checksum for that exact release. Do not reuse this checksum for a different version.

### Extract the verified package

I extracted the package to inspect its bundled installation scripts:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-inspect

dpkg-deb -x ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb \
  ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-inspect
```

I checked the build information:

```bash
cat ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-inspect/usr/lib/cindy/resources/linux-build-info
```

The output was:

```text
cindy-linux-v1
0.1.121
x64
global
Cindy
```

## 4. Backing Up Existing User Data

Before migrating, I closed Cindy and backed up the existing configuration and user data.

My backup command was:

```bash
BACKUP="$HOME/Downloads/cindy-backup-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).tar.gz"

tar -czf "$BACKUP" \
  -C "$HOME" \
  .config/CindyGlobal \
  .config/Cindy \
  .local/share/Cindy \
  .local/share/applications/cindy.desktop \
  .local/share/icons/cindy.png

echo "Backup created at: $BACKUP"
ls -lh "$BACKUP"
```

The paths above reflect the data and launcher locations found on my system. Your installation may use different paths, so inspect your own setup before using the same command.

Keep the backup until you have verified the new installation and your existing data.

## 5. Installing Cindy with the Bundled User Installer

I ran the installer from the verified 0.1.121 package and selected a separate installation directory:

```bash
bash ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-inspect/usr/lib/cindy/resources/linux/install-user.sh \
  --install ~/Downloads/cindy-0.1.121-amd64.deb \
  b756663c3f30d6cf8a75d4d8ea38336842689a504b682d62c6dd3d6ba01e0663 \
  "$HOME/.local/opt/cindy-managed"
```

The installer completed successfully:

```text
Installed Cindy 0.1.121.
Start with: /home/lporcsin/.local/opt/cindy-managed/launch
```

The installation was created under:

```text
~/.local/opt/cindy-managed
```

**Important:** Run the user installer as your regular desktop user. Do not run it with `sudo`.

Using a separate directory allowed me to keep the original manually extracted installation available while checking the new one.

## 6. Registering Cindy in the GNOME Application Menu

I registered the managed installation using Cindy's bundled desktop-registration script:

```bash
bash "$HOME/.local/opt/cindy-managed/current/resources/linux/register-desktop.sh" \
  "$HOME/.local/opt/cindy-managed"
```

Then I launched Cindy:

```bash
"$HOME/.local/opt/cindy-managed/launch"
```

**Result:** Cindy 0.1.121 launched successfully from the managed user installation.

## 7. Fixing Duplicate Cindy Icons in GNOME

After registering the new installation, GNOME displayed two launchers:

- **Cindy** — my manually created launcher pointing to the extracted application.
- **Cindy (User)** — the launcher registered for the managed user installation.

The duplicate appeared because the old custom desktop entry was still present.

After verifying that the new installation worked, I removed only the old manually created desktop entry:

```bash
rm ~/.local/share/applications/cindy.desktop
```

This command removes the desktop entry, not the application profile or user data.

I kept the **Cindy (User)** launcher.

If the old icon remains visible temporarily, refresh the GNOME session or log out and back in. Do not delete other launchers unless you have verified which installation they point to.

## 8. Notes and Troubleshooting

### The `.deb` package does not install like a native Fedora RPM

The initial package was extracted rather than installed system-wide. This allowed me to test whether the application would launch, but it did not provide the managed installation and update workflow.

### The updater rejected the manually extracted installation

Cindy reported that the installation could not update itself. Migrating to the bundled user installer resolved the installation-method issue by creating a managed user installation.

### `bsdtar` was missing

Installing the Fedora package that provides `bsdtar` satisfied this prerequisite.

### Two Cindy icons appeared

The duplicate was caused by the old manually created desktop entry remaining alongside the new registered launcher.

### Protect your profile and credentials

Before migrating:

- Close Cindy.
- Back up the existing configuration and user data.
- Do not run two installations simultaneously against the same profile.
- Keep the old installation and backup until the new one has been verified.
- Do not delete Cindy's profile, keyring data, or stored credentials as part of routine launcher cleanup.

## Final Result

I successfully launched Cindy 0.1.120 from an extracted Debian package on Fedora 44 Workstation and then migrated to Cindy 0.1.121 using the user installer bundled with the verified official package.

The managed installation launched successfully and was registered in the GNOME application menu. I also resolved the duplicate launcher caused by my earlier manual setup.

This guide records what worked on my system. Other Fedora configurations and future Cindy releases may require different steps, so consult the [official Linux installation guide](https://github.com/makecindy/cindy/blob/main/docs/linux.md) before attempting a new installation or upgrade.
